Baseline PBR shading, atmosphere and surface detail
==================================================

Scope and shared contracts
  Improve baseline physically based rendering with and without LumOn. Provide a physically based
  sky, optional height/depth-map tessellation and relief mapping, PBR liquids, an investigation of
  approximate underwater caustics, and volumetric clouds.
  Keep standalone PBR usable without LumOn resources or indirect-lighting production. Standalone
  environmental lighting should keep outdoor shadows readable without injecting sunlight through
  occluders or applying an indiscriminate brightness floor to enclosed spaces.
  These are implementation tasks; algorithm choices and performance targets require investigation.
  Reuse existing shader/resource owners, compiled SPIR-V contracts, demand loading and settings.
  Keep optional features bounded, configurable and independently disableable.
  This list owns baseline PBR material improvements. Surface Cache performance
  and geometry work remain in docs/LumOn.SurfaceCache.PerformanceAndMaterials.todo.

Tasks

  ☐ Profile and optimize ambient-occlusion GPU cost while preserving visibility quality.
    - Bounds-arithmetic candidates were rejected: uncontended captured-scene horizon cost improved
      about 13-15%, but full-resolution synthetic cases consistently regressed about 10-50%.
      Original production bounds are restored; captured-input and synthetic comparison fixtures remain.
      The reference reproduces event 7091's original output bit-for-bit. Two full bounds replacements
      passed exact-image checks but failed performance qualification; the ceiling-only variant passed
      22 image cases and was not timed before changing investigation direction.
      Evaluate reducing hierarchy source-recovery fetches, including per-parent minimum-child masks
      that preserve query-dependent ties. Measure added build/storage cost and the full AO path before
      selecting an implementation. Matched live scenes, both lighting modes and user acceptance remain open.
      Implementation/evidence: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md / Horizon-integrated ambient visibility.
    - Starting evidence: the 2026-10-11 performance-issue.rdc capture at 3440x1377 has repeatable
      AO replay timings of 2.71-2.72 ms, dominated by the half-resolution horizon pass at event 7091
      (~2.61 ms). These are optimization leads, not proof of the reported 120+ to 90 FPS regression;
      inconsistent engine-draw replay spikes invalidate total replay frame-time comparisons.
      Extracted evidence: artifacts/RenderDocPerformance/timings_repeat_*.json and pipelines.json.
    - Establish matched steady-state CPU/GPU and live frame-time baselines across native AO quality,
      resolution, representative outdoor/interior/foliage scenes and LumOn enabled/disabled. Record
      settings, hardware and build configuration; measure shared DepthHierarchy once, then horizon,
      filtering and reconstruction separately. Compare AO enabled/disabled to isolate its contribution.
    - Profile horizon candidate sampling, hierarchy descent, texture bandwidth and integration work;
      evaluate reuse, locality and redundant-work removal before selecting an optimization. Reuse
      existing AO, depth-hierarchy, shader and resource owners; retain native quality/enable authority.
    - Preserve running-horizon relaxation, projection and receiver-depth agreement, transmission,
      background rejection, edge-aware reconstruction and indirect-only lighting application.
      Any sampling/resolution tradeoff must be explicit, quality-bounded and visually accepted;
      do not count a silent reduction in quality as an equivalent-work speedup.
    - Validate changed behavior through focused subagent numerical/GPU tests and independent review;
      obtain user-run visual acceptance for contact shadows, thin geometry, foliage, screen edges,
      movement and disocclusion. Check resize, reload, runtime settings and both lighting modes.
    - Record matched before/after pass and live frame costs, bandwidth/storage changes and remaining
      limits. Remove temporary profiling instrumentation; mark complete only with measured improvement
      and preserved output contracts. Contract: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md / Horizon-integrated ambient visibility.

    Optimization evaluations
      Each evaluation inherits the parent task's settings, lifecycle, lighting and acceptance contracts.
      Evaluate alternatives independently against the frozen reference before combining successful changes.
      Record measured costs, output differences and a retain/reject decision; an evaluation may finish
      with rejection, but the parent optimization task still requires a measured, accepted improvement.

      ☐ Evaluate reuse of repeated sample lookups within each AO receiver.
        - Measure duplicate rates first. Reuse source recovery only for equivalent quantized sample
          coordinates, footprint levels and inputs; preserve ordered horizon updates for every sample.
        - Compare saved reads against lookup overhead, added registers and spills. Keep reuse bounded
          and local to the current receiver; validate bit-identical output and total pass cost.

      ☐ Evaluate horizon integration math using vector projections.
        - Investigate eliminating redundant angle/trigonometric conversions while preserving signed
          horizon selection, limits, relaxation, transmission weighting and integration behavior.
        - Cover grazing angles, zero/near-zero vectors, horizon boundaries and both quality settings.
          Quantify numerical and complete-image differences against the reference; define and justify
          any nonzero tolerance explicitly and obtain visual acceptance before retaining changed output.

      ☐ Evaluate spatially tiled AO processing for data reuse and locality.
        - Measure depth/material locality and footprint reach before selecting tile size or execution
          layout. Account for wide footprints, tile halos, odd extents, boundaries and background work.
        - Compare shared-memory/register pressure, occupancy, synchronization and extra preparation
          against saved reads. Preserve sampling and output contracts; include every added pass in
          timing and test hardware limits and resource lifecycle through the existing owners.

      ☐ Evaluate adaptive sampling with explicit visibility-quality bounds.
        - Investigate reducing samples in smooth regions while retaining evidence near discontinuities;
          validate the classifier itself against thin geometry, foliage, corners and distant receivers.
        - Measure classification overhead and complete AO cost against the fixed-sample reference.
          Record quality differences, spatial instability and missed occluders; require user acceptance
          and retain native quality authority. Do not report reduced sampling as equivalent-work savings.

      ☐ Evaluate temporal accumulation with reduced per-frame sampling.
        - Assess motion/reprojection inputs and history ownership before selecting a design. Include
          depth/normal rejection, disocclusion, camera cuts, moving geometry and confidence handling.
        - Measure history storage, reprojection/filter cost and total AO savings; check ghosting,
          flicker, convergence and responsiveness against the current-frame reference in motion.
        - Specify invalidation for resize, reload, world changes and runtime settings, plus behavior
          when valid history is unavailable. Require explicit visual acceptance of the temporal tradeoff
          and reconcile the current no-history contract in docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md before adoption.

  ☐ Optimize owned postprocessing submission and pass scheduling.
    - Starting evidence: the same capture's postprocessing interval contains 25 fullscreen draws,
      54 program switches, 108 sampler binds and 75 framebuffer binds, with ~0.28 ms repeatable GPU
      replay cost. Binding churn predates the shader lifecycle ownership refactor; its managed CPU
      cost and contribution to the FPS regression remain unmeasured. Evidence:
      artifacts/RenderDocPerformance/frame_events.json and timings_repeat_*.json.
    - Establish matched CPU submission/allocation, GPU and live frame-time baselines. Attribute costs
      to PostprocessDraw preparation/submission, shader activation, input publication, context checks,
      pass teardown and boundary restoration. Compare equivalent builds before/after the lifecycle
      refactor where reproducible; separate first-use preparation and diagnostics from steady state.
    - Reduce measured redundant program/sampler/framebuffer transitions through existing command,
      pipeline and state-cache owners. Evaluate retaining compatible state across owned passes and
      restoring engine state at the enclosing boundary; preserve complete restoration on normal,
      exception, nested and unavailable-resource paths and safe executable retirement.
    - Evaluate bloom reduction/filter/reconstruction scheduling and intermediate bandwidth for
      measured opportunities to reuse work or combine compatible passes. Preserve the selected kernel,
      scale weights and output quality; skip disabled-effect work through existing settings authority.
    - Preserve scene-linear HDR, exposure metering/adaptation order, bloom and shaft behavior, grading,
      exactly-once display conversion/dithering and UI/offscreen separation. Keep attachment ownership,
      feedback avoidance, resize/reload and both PBR lighting modes intact; coordinate shared changes
      with the AO task above and the water performance task below without duplicating their work.
    - Use focused subagent state-restoration, image/numerical and lifecycle tests plus independent
      review. Obtain user-run visual acceptance; record matched call counts, CPU/GPU/frame costs and
      allocation/storage changes. Do not use reduced call counts alone as completion evidence.
      Contract: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md; retain results there and remove temporary diagnostics.

  ☐ Revisit the bounded depth-sorted transparency proposal after photometric lighting qualification.
